\strong{Survival outcomes.} \code{weibullSurvFamily()} and \code{exponentialSurvFamily()}
model a time to event with right censoring, written as
\code{time | event(status) ~ ...} (\code{status} 1 = the event was observed at \code{time},
0 = censored at \code{time}), one row per subject. INLA's \code{weibullsurv} (variant 0)
has hazard \eqn{h(t) = \alpha \lambda t^{\alpha - 1}} and survival
\eqn{S(t) = \exp(-\lambda t^\alpha)} with \eqn{\log \lambda} the linear
predictor (the exponential is \eqn{\alpha = 1}), so an association
coefficient is a log hazard ratio. The marker enters the hazard at the values
of its covariates given in the outcome row (e.g. \code{t = time}). Survival
responses are not plotted, have no residuals and cannot be predicted; their
fitted value (\code{\link[=fittedValues]{fittedValues()}}) is \eqn{\lambda} (the hazard scale), not a
mean time. A time of 0 is accepted for a censored row only.

\strong{Hyperparameter priors.} A hyperparameter argument is \code{NULL} (INLA's
default prior), a prior constructor (see \link{priors}) or a raw INLA \code{hyper}
entry, e.g. \code{list(prior = "loggamma", param = c(1, 0.01))} or
\code{list(initial = log(100), fixed = TRUE)}. The constructors are accepted for
precision-like parameters (\code{prec}, \code{phi}: \code{\link[=gammaPrec]{gammaPrec()}}, \code{\link[=pcSd]{pcSd()}},
\code{\link[=halfNormalSd]{halfNormalSd()}}, \code{\link[=halfCauchySd]{halfCauchySd()}}, placing the prior on the parameter
\eqn{\tau} or on \eqn{\tau^{-1/2}}) and, for \code{size}, \code{\link[=gammaPrec]{gammaPrec()}}
(\eqn{\kappa \sim \mathrm{Gamma}}); other parameters take raw INLA entries.

\strong{Inputs on the left-hand side.} Binomial-type families take the number of
trials and count families an exposure (INLA's \code{Ntrials} / \code{E}), given in the
formula as in brms: \code{y | trials(n) ~ ...}, \code{y | exposure(pt) ~ ...} (any
expression of the data in the parentheses). Without \code{trials()} the binomial
is a Bernoulli (\eqn{n = 1}); without \code{exposure()}, \eqn{E = 1}.

\strong{Observation weights.} \code{y | weights(w) ~ ...} (INLA's \code{scale}; positive
and finite) multiplies the precision of observation \eqn{i} by \eqn{w_i}:
gaussian and Student-t \eqn{\varepsilon_i \sim N(0, (w_i \tau_\varepsilon)^{-1})}
(e.g. \eqn{w_i} = the inverse of a known relative measurement variance), gamma
and beta \eqn{\phi_i = w_i \phi}. These are the likelihoods whose INLA
documentation lists \code{scale} (\code{INLA::inla.doc("gaussian")}, \code{"T"}, \code{"gamma"},
\code{"beta"}); \code{lognormal} does not document it, so \code{lognormalFamily()} does not
take \code{weights()}. \code{inlaFamily()} passes \code{weights()} on unchecked: INLA ignores
\code{scale} for likelihoods that do not use it. Without \code{weights()}, \eqn{w_i = 1}.
These are not likelihood weights (INLA's \code{weights}, which multiply the
log-likelihood). They can be combined with another input of the same family
(\code{inlaFamily()}: \code{y | trials(n) + weights(w) ~ ...}).

Responses are checked against the support of the family by \code{\link[=prepareData]{prepareData()}}.
